# see 1.5 at all. It asked for this array by name.
#
# One entry per minor that corrected a meaning; a pure key addition adds
# nothing here, so an empty list is the normal case and says so honestly. A
# consumer records the minor it read against and reports every entry newer than
# that, which is why this is a list rather than "what changed in the current
# version" — a front-end that skips 1.5 → 1.7 must still learn about 1.5.
